window.QUIZ_DATA = {"course": "Data Science Quiz", "topics": {"02": {"title": "Probability Foundations", "cards": [{"ID": "s2-01", "Topic": "2.1 · Random Variables", "Question": "What is a random variable?", "Short": "<p>A random variable is a measurable function that assigns a real number to each outcome of a random experiment. Measurability ensures that statements about its values correspond to events to which we can assign probabilities.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable turns the outcome of an experiment into a numerical value. Formally, it is a measurable function from a sample space to the real numbers. I write this mapping as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb{P})\\)<\/span> is the probability space and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal B\\)<\/span> is the Borel sigma-algebra on the real numbers. Measurable means that the preimage of every Borel set is an event in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal F\\)<\/span>. In particular, for every real number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, the set of outcomes for which <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\le x\\)<\/span> is an event, so its probability is defined.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"intuition\" class=\"unnumbered\">Intuition<\/h3>\n<p>The randomness is in the outcome <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\omega\\in\\Omega\\)<\/span>; once <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span> is fixed, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X(\\omega)\\)<\/span> is just a deterministic number.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Toss two coins. Let<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\Omega=\\{HH,HT,TH,TT\\}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> as the number of heads. Then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X(HH)=2,\\qquad X(HT)=X(TH)=1,\\qquad X(TT)=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H\\)<\/span> means <em>heads<\/em> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\)<\/span> means <em>tails<\/em>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.1.1)<\/strong> X is a measurable function from the sample space Omega, with sigma-algebra script F, to the real numbers, with the Borel sigma-algebra script B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.1.2)<\/strong> Omega is the set containing heads-heads, heads-tails, tails-heads, and tails-tails.<\/p><p><strong>(2.1.3)<\/strong> X of heads-heads equals two; X of heads-tails and X of tails-heads both equal one; X of tails-tails equals zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-02", "Topic": "2.2 · PMF, PDF, and CDF", "Question": "What are a PMF, a PDF, and a CDF?", "Short": "<p>A probability mass function gives the probability of each possible value of a discrete random variable. A probability density function is a nonnegative function whose integral over an interval gives the probability of falling in that interval. A cumulative distribution function gives the probability that a random variable is less than or equal to a specified value.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A probability mass function describes a discrete distribution by assigning a probability to each possible value. If I denote it by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_X\\)<\/span>, its definition is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p_X(x)=\\mathbb{P}(X=x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>It satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_X(x)\\ge0\\)<\/span> and<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\sum_x p_X(x)=1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A probability density function describes an absolutely continuous distribution through areas under a nonnegative curve. The density itself is not a point probability. Instead, integrating it over an interval gives the probability that the random variable falls in that interval, which I write as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(a&lt;X\\le b)=\\int_a^b f_X(x)\\,dx.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>It satisfies<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f_X(x)\\,dx=1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a continuous random variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(X=x)=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>for every single point <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>. A density value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_X(x)\\)<\/span> is therefore not itself a point probability, and a density may exceed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> on a sufficiently short interval.<\/p>\n<p>A cumulative distribution function works for any random variable, including discrete and continuous ones. It accumulates probability up to a threshold x, including the threshold itself. Its definition is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_X(x)=\\mathbb{P}(X\\le x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a continuous random variable with density,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_X(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,dt,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Conversely, differentiating the CDF recovers its density almost everywhere; in particular, equality holds wherever the chosen density is continuous:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[f_X(x)=F&#39;_X(x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>A continuous CDF need not have a density. In these notes, the continuous distribution families used for calculations are absolutely continuous.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.2.1)<\/strong> Lowercase p sub X of x equals the probability that capital X equals lowercase x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.2)<\/strong> The sum of p sub X of x over all possible values of x equals one.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.3)<\/strong> The probability that X is greater than a and less than or equal to b equals the integral from a to b of f sub X of x, d x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.4)<\/strong> The integral from negative infinity to infinity of f sub X of x, d x, equals one.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.5)<\/strong> The probability that X equals any particular value x is zero.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.6)<\/strong> Capital F sub X of x equals the probability that X is less than or equal to x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.7)<\/strong> F sub X of x equals the integral from negative infinity to x of f sub X of t, d t.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.8)<\/strong> The density f sub X of x equals the derivative of the CDF at x, almost everywhere.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-03", "Topic": "2.3 · Expectation, Moments, and Variance", "Question": "What are expectation, raw and central moments, variance, and standard deviation?", "Short": "<p>The expected value is the probability-weighted average of a random variable. Raw moments are expected powers of that variable, while central moments are expected powers of its deviation from its mean. Variance is the second central moment: the expected squared deviation from the mean. Standard deviation is the square root of variance.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The expected value describes the center of a distribution by weighting possible values according to their probabilities. For an integrable random variable, its general definition is an integral with respect to the probability measure:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\int_{\\Omega}X(\\omega)\\,d\\mathbb{P}(\\omega).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a discrete random variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\sum_x x\\mathbb{P}(X=x),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and for a continuous random variable with density,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x f_X(x)\\,dx.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This probability-weighted center is also the first raw moment. Moments extend the same averaging idea to powers of a random variable.<\/p>\n<p>The k-th raw moment averages the k-th power of X, so I define it by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[m_k=\\mathbb{E}[X^k],\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)<\/span>-th central moment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mu_k=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^k].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[m_1=\\mathbb{E}[X].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Variance measures spread around the expected value. It is the second central moment, so the entire deviation is squared before taking the expectation:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2].}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Expanding the square gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[X^2]-\\mathbb{E}[X]^2.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The standard deviation is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{SD}(X)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>These definitions explain how shifting or rescaling a random variable changes its mean and spread. For constants a and b, expectation transforms as follows:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[aX+b]=a\\mathbb{E}[X]+b,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>More generally, expectation is linear:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_iX_i\\right] =\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\\mathbb{E}[X_i],\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>without any independence assumption.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.3.1)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the integral over Omega of X of omega with respect to the probability measure P.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.2)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the sum, over all possible values x, of x times the probability that X equals x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.3)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the integral from negative infinity to infinity of x times f sub X of x, d x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.4)<\/strong> The k-th raw moment of X is the expected value of X to the power of k.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.5)<\/strong> The k-th central moment of X is the expected value of the quantity X minus its expected value, raised to the power of k.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.6)<\/strong> M sub one equals the expected value of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.7)<\/strong> The variance of X is the expected value of the squared difference between X and its mean.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.8)<\/strong> The variance of X equals the expected value of X squared minus the square of the expected value of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.9)<\/strong> The standard deviation of X is the square root of its variance.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.10)<\/strong> The expected value of a times X plus b equals a times the expected value of X plus b.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.11)<\/strong> The variance of a times X plus b equals a squared times the variance of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.12)<\/strong> The expected value of the sum, from i equals one to n, of a sub i times X sub i equals the sum, over the same indices, of a sub i times the expected value of X sub i.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-04", "Topic": "2.5 · Conditional Probability", "Question": "What is conditional probability?", "Short": "<p>Conditional probability is the probability of an event after restricting attention to outcomes where another event has occurred.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Conditional probability renormalizes probabilities within the conditioning event. If B has positive probability, the probability of A given B is the probability of their intersection divided by the probability of B:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\frac{\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathbb{P}(B)}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.5.1)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of A and B divided by the probability of B, provided that the probability of B is positive.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-05", "Topic": "2.5 · Bayes' Theorem", "Question": "What is Bayes’ theorem?", "Short": "<p>Bayes’ theorem expresses the probability of A given B in terms of the probability of B given A and the marginal probabilities of A and B. It lets us update a prior probability after observing evidence.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Bayes’ theorem describes how to update the probability of an event A after observing an event B. The prior probability of A is multiplied by the probability of observing B given A, then divided by the overall probability of B. Assuming <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A)&gt;0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>, I write this as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A)\\mathbb{P}(A)}{\\mathbb{P}(B)}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"derivation\" class=\"unnumbered\">Derivation<\/h3>\n<p>Bayes’ theorem follows by rewriting the numerator using the definition of conditional probability in the opposite direction. The same joint probability has two equivalent expressions:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)\\mathbb{P}(B)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A)\\mathbb{P}(A).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating these two expressions and dividing by the probability of B gives Bayes’ theorem.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>I can also expand the denominator using the law of total probability. If the events <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_1,\\dots,A_k\\)<\/span> partition the sample space and the conditional probabilities used are defined, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(B)=\\sum_{j=1}^k\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_j)\\mathbb{P}(A_j),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(A_i\\mid B)= \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_i)\\mathbb{P}(A_i)} {\\sum_{j=1}^k\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_j)\\mathbb{P}(A_j)}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.6)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.5.4)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of B given A times the probability of A, all divided by the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.2)<\/strong> The probability of A and B equals the probability of A given B times the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.3)<\/strong> The probability of both A and B can be written as the probability of A given B times the probability of B, or as the probability of B given A times the probability of A.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.5)<\/strong> The probability of B is the sum, from j equals one to k, of the probability of B given A sub j times the probability of A sub j.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.6)<\/strong> The probability of A sub i given B equals the probability of B given A sub i times the probability of A sub i, divided by the sum, from j equals one to k, of the probability of B given A sub j times the probability of A sub j.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-06", "Topic": "2.6 · Independent Events", "Question": "What are independent events?", "Short": "<p>Independent events are events whose joint probability equals the product of their individual probabilities. When the conditioning event has positive probability, knowing that it occurred does not change the probability of the other event.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Independence means that the joint probability factorizes into the product of the two marginal probabilities. This gives a definition that also works for zero-probability events:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(A)\\mathbb{P}(B).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>, this is equivalent to<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\mathbb{P}(A).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.6.1)<\/strong> A and B are independent if the probability of both A and B equals the probability of A times the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.6.2)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of A.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-07", "Topic": "2.6 · Mutually Exclusive Events", "Question": "What are mutually exclusive events?", "Short": "<p>Mutually exclusive, or disjoint, events are events that cannot occur together: they have no outcomes in common.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Disjointness concerns which outcomes the events contain, rather than how their probabilities factorize. Events A and B are disjoint if their intersection is empty:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{A\\cap B=\\varnothing.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Then necessarily<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The converse need not hold in a general probability space because nonempty events can have probability zero.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)<\/span> are disjoint and both have positive probability, they cannot be independent, because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0\\)<\/span> whereas <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A)\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.6.3)<\/strong> A intersection B is the empty set. In words, A and B have no outcomes in common.<\/p><p><strong>(2.6.4)<\/strong> The probability of A intersection B is zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-08", "Topic": "2.7 · Covariance and Correlation", "Question": "What are covariance and correlation?", "Short": "<p>Covariance is the expected product of two random variables’ deviations from their respective expected values. Pearson correlation is covariance divided by the product of their standard deviations. Correlation measures linear association on a scale from minus one to one.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Covariance measures whether two variables tend to deviate from their expected values in the same or opposite directions. Assuming finite second moments, I calculate it as the expected product of those deviations:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])(Y-\\mathbb{E}[Y])] =\\mathbb{E}[XY]-\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[Y]. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Covariance measures linear co-movement but depends on scale. Its physical units are the product of the units of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Pearson correlation removes the dependence on measurement units by normalizing covariance. Specifically, I divide by the product of the standard deviations:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\rho_{X,Y}=\\operatorname{Corr}(X,Y) =\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y}, }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>provided <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_X,\\sigma_Y&gt;0\\)<\/span>. It is dimensionless and satisfies<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[-1\\le\\rho_{X,Y}\\le1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\)<\/span> and the required moments exist, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and therefore <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Corr}(X,Y)=0\\)<\/span> when both variances are positive. The converse is false in general.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(-1,1)\\)<\/span> and define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X^2\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> is completely determined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, so the variables are dependent. However,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb{E}[X^3]-\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[X^2]=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>by symmetry. Thus zero correlation only excludes linear association, not general dependence.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up-1\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X,Y)\\)<\/span> is jointly Gaussian, zero covariance is sufficient for independence.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.7.1)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y is the expected product of their deviations from their respective means. Equivalently, it is the expected value of X times Y minus the product of their expected values.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.2)<\/strong> The correlation of X and Y is their covariance divided by the product of their standard deviations.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.3)<\/strong> Rho sub X Y lies between minus one and one, inclusive.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.4)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.5)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y equals the expected value of X cubed minus the expected value of X times the expected value of X squared. By symmetry, this equals zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-10", "Topic": "2.9 · Law of Large Numbers", "Question": "What is the law of large numbers?", "Short": "<p>The law of large numbers says that the sample mean converges to the population mean as the sample size grows. In the weak law for independent and identically distributed observations with a finite mean, this is convergence in probability.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The weak law of large numbers describes where the sample mean goes as the sample grows. For iid observations with a finite mean mu, the result is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\bar X_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\\xrightarrow{P}\\mu. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus the sample mean is a consistent estimator of the population mean.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.9.2)<\/strong> X bar sub n is one over n times the sum of X sub i from i equals one to n. It converges in probability to mu.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-11", "Topic": "2.9 · Central Limit Theorem", "Question": "What is the central limit theorem?", "Short": "<p>The central limit theorem says that, for independent and identically distributed observations with positive finite variance, the centered and properly scaled sample mean converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The central limit theorem describes the shape and scale of fluctuations around that mean. For independent and identically distributed observations, I assume<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X_i]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X_i)=\\sigma^2\\in(0,\\infty),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i-n\\mu}{\\sigma\\sqrt n} \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0,1). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equivalently,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar X_n-\\mu)}{\\sigma}\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal{N}(0,1). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> this motivates the approximation<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\bar X_n\\approx\\mathcal{N}\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The convergence used here is convergence in distribution. It means that the CDFs converge to the limiting CDF:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_{Y_n}(x)\\to F_Y(x)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>at every continuity point of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_Y\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>The LLN concerns concentration around the mean; the CLT concerns the distribution of scaled estimation error. The CLT explains why approximately Gaussian sampling distributions appear throughout estimation, confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, and A/B experiments even when individual observations are not Gaussian.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.9.3)<\/strong> Each X sub i has mean mu and variance sigma squared, which is strictly positive and finite.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.4)<\/strong> The sum of X sub i from i equals one to n, minus n times mu, all divided by sigma times the square root of n, converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.5)<\/strong> The square root of n times the quantity X bar sub n minus mu, all divided by sigma, converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.6)<\/strong> For large n, X bar sub n is approximately normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.7)<\/strong> The CDF of Y sub n, evaluated at x, converges to the CDF of Y, evaluated at x, at every continuity point of the limiting CDF.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}]}, "03": {"title": "Probability Distributions", "cards": [{"ID": "s3-01", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.1 · Bernoulli Distribution", "Question": "What is a Bernoulli distribution?", "Short": "<p>A random variable follows a Bernoulli distribution with parameter p if it takes the value one with probability p and zero with probability one minus p. It models one binary outcome; its mean is p and its variance is p times one minus p.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a Bernoulli distribution when it takes only the values zero and one, with probabilities one minus p and p, respectively. To connect that definition to an experiment, I call its two outcomes failure and success. Such an experiment is a Bernoulli trial, and its numerical outcome is the indicator<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X=\\begin{cases} 1,&amp;\\text{success},\\\\ 0,&amp;\\text{failure}. \\end{cases}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I denote the resulting distribution by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{X\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)},\\qquad 0\\le p\\le1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its support is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)<\/span> and its PMF is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(X=x)=p^x(1-p)^{1-x},\\qquad x\\in\\{0,1\\}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The mean is the probability of success, because only the value one contributes to the expectation. The mean and variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=p,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Indeed, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X^2=X\\)<\/span> for a Bernoulli variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[X^2]-\\mathbb{E}[X]^2=p-p^2=p(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Click/no click, default/no default, fraud/no fraud, and conversion/no conversion are natural Bernoulli outcomes.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For iid <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_1,\\dots,X_n\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=p.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating the theoretical first moment with the sample first moment gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To derive the maximum likelihood estimator, I treat the observations as fixed and multiply their Bernoulli probabilities under independence:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)=\\prod_{i=1}^n p^{x_i}(1-p)^{1-x_i}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=\\sum_i x_i\\)<\/span>. Then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)=p^S(1-p)^{n-S},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(p)=S\\log p+(n-S)\\log(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(p)=\\frac{S}{p}-\\frac{n-S}{1-p}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an interior maximum, I set this derivative equal to zero and solve for p. This gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S(1-p)=p(n-S)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad S=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\frac Sn=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.14)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0&lt;S&lt;n\\)<\/span> the derivative gives an interior maximum. If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=n\\)<\/span>, maximize on the boundary to obtain <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat p=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, respectively. Thus the MoM and MLE estimates coincide.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.1.1)<\/strong> X equals one for success and zero for failure.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.2)<\/strong> X follows a Bernoulli distribution with success probability p, where p is between zero and one, inclusive.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.3)<\/strong> The probability that X equals x is p to the power of x times the quantity one minus p raised to the power of one minus x, for x equal to zero or one.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.4)<\/strong> The expected value of X equals p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.5)<\/strong> The expected value of X is p, and the variance of X is p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.6)<\/strong> The variance of X equals the expected value of X squared minus the square of its expected value. For Bernoulli X, this becomes p minus p squared, or p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.7)<\/strong> The first theoretical moment is the expected value of X, which equals p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.8)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate p hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.9)<\/strong> L of p is the product, from i equals one to n, of p to the power x sub i times the quantity one minus p raised to the power one minus x sub i.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.10)<\/strong> L of p equals p to the power S times the quantity one minus p raised to the power n minus S.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.11)<\/strong> Ell of p equals S times log p plus the quantity n minus S times log of one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.12)<\/strong> Ell prime of p equals S divided by p, minus the quantity n minus S divided by the quantity one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.13)<\/strong> S times one minus p equals p times the quantity n minus S. Rearranging gives S equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.14)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate p hat equals S divided by n, which is X bar: the observed success rate.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-02", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.2 · Binomial Distribution", "Question": "What is a binomial distribution?", "Short": "<p>A random variable follows a binomial distribution with parameters n and p if it counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials with the same success probability p. Its possible values are zero through n, its mean is n times p, and its variance is n times p times one minus p.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A binomial distribution describes the number of successes in a fixed number of independent trials with the same probability of success. I construct the count from iid Bernoulli variables:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_1,\\dots,X_n\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The number of successes<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S_n=\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>has a binomial distribution:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{S_n\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The count takes values from zero to n. To obtain exactly k successes, I choose their positions in n choose k ways and multiply by the probability of any one such arrangement. Therefore its probability mass function is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(S_n=k)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Linearity of expectation gives the mean, and independence lets me add the variances of the individual trials. Consequently,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[S_n]=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(S_n)=np(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>The binomial construction requires a fixed number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> of trials, binary outcomes, a common success probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, and independence across trials. These assumptions are part of the construction rather than a separate definition.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(10{,}000\\)<\/span> independently exposed users each convert with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, the total number of conversions is approximately or exactly binomial depending on the experimental design assumptions.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For estimation, suppose I observe m independent binomial counts, each based on n trials, where n is known. The theoretical first moment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[Y]=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>we obtain<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\frac{\\bar Y}{n}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate p by maximum likelihood, I multiply the probability masses for those observed counts. Terms that do not depend on p can be omitted, giving<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)\\propto\\prod_{j=1}^m p^{y_j}(1-p)^{n-y_j}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Ignoring factors independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(p)=\\left(\\sum_jy_j\\right)\\log p+ \\left(mn-\\sum_jy_j\\right)\\log(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating and setting to zero gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat p_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac{\\sum_jy_j}{mn} =\\frac{\\bar Y}{n}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If all trials fail or all succeed, the maximum is at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span>; the same formula applies. Again MLE and MoM coincide.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.2.1)<\/strong> X sub one through X sub n are independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables with success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.2)<\/strong> S sub n equals the sum of X sub i from i equals one to n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.3)<\/strong> S sub n follows a binomial distribution with n trials and success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.4)<\/strong> The probability that S sub n equals k is n choose k times p to the power of k times the quantity one minus p raised to the power of n minus k.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.5)<\/strong> The expected value of S sub n equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.6)<\/strong> The expected number of successes is n times p, and the variance is n times p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.7)<\/strong> The expected value of Y equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.8)<\/strong> P hat equals Y bar divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.9)<\/strong> The likelihood is proportional to the product, from j equals one to m, of p to the power y sub j times the quantity one minus p raised to the power n minus y sub j.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.10)<\/strong> The log-likelihood is the sum of the observed counts times log p, plus the quantity m times n minus that sum, multiplied by log of one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.11)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of p is the total number of successes divided by m times n, or Y bar divided by n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-03", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.3 · Poisson Distribution", "Question": "What is a Poisson distribution?", "Short": "<p>A Poisson distribution is a discrete distribution over nonnegative integer counts with parameter lambda. The probability of count k is e to the negative lambda times lambda to the power of k, divided by k factorial. Its mean and variance both equal lambda.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a Poisson distribution with positive parameter lambda if it takes nonnegative integer values with the following probability mass function:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(X=k)=e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}, \\qquad k=0,1,2,\\dots }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and we write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The parameter lambda determines both the center and spread of the count distribution. Its defining moment relationship is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\lambda,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\lambda.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"intuition\" class=\"unnumbered\">Intuition<\/h3>\n<p><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> is the expected number of events in the chosen observation window. If an event process has physical rate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)<\/span> events per unit time and the window has length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\lambda=rt.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)<\/span> has units <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/\\text{time}\\)<\/span>, while <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> is an expected count and therefore dimensionless as a pure number.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If calls arrive at an average rate of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=12\\)<\/span> per hour, then the expected count in half an hour is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda=12\\cdot0.5=6\\)<\/span> and a Poisson model would write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(N(0.5)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(6)\\)<\/span> under suitable assumptions.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"poisson-limit-theorem.\">Poisson limit theorem.<\/h4>\n<p>The Poisson distribution also arises as a rare-event limit of binomial counts. To explain this, I start with<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_n\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p_n).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ n\\to\\infty,\\qquad p_n\\to0,\\qquad np_n\\to\\lambda\\in(0,\\infty), }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{X_n\\xrightarrow{d}\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For each fixed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{P}(X_n=k) =e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A canonical scaling is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_n=\\lambda/n\\)<\/span> for sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span>: each individual opportunity becomes rarer while the number of opportunities grows so that the expected count <span class=\"math inline\">\\(np_n\\)<\/span> remains finite.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\lambda\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate lambda from iid counts, I write the likelihood as the product of their Poisson probability masses:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\lambda)=\\prod_{i=1}^n e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^{x_i}}{x_i!}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\lambda) =-n\\lambda+\\left(\\sum_i x_i\\right)\\log\\lambda- \\sum_i\\log(x_i!).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(\\lambda)=-n+\\frac{\\sum_i x_i}{\\lambda}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>With the stated parameter space <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&gt;0\\)<\/span>, an all-zero sample has no attained MLE: the likelihood approaches its supremum as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>. If the model includes the degenerate distribution at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda=0\\)<\/span>, the MLE is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bar X=0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.4.1)<\/strong> The probability that X equals k is e to the power of negative lambda, times lambda to the power of k, divided by k factorial. K can be any nonnegative integer.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.2)<\/strong> X follows a Poisson distribution with parameter lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.3)<\/strong> The expected value and the variance of X both equal lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.4)<\/strong> Lambda equals the event rate r times the duration t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.5)<\/strong> X sub n follows a binomial distribution with n trials and success probability p sub n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.6)<\/strong> N tends to infinity, p sub n tends to zero, and n times p sub n tends to a positive finite value lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.7)<\/strong> X sub n converges in distribution to a Poisson random variable with parameter lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.8)<\/strong> As n tends to infinity, the probability that X sub n equals k tends to e to the power negative lambda times lambda to the power k, divided by k factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.9)<\/strong> Lambda hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.10)<\/strong> L of lambda is the product, from i equals one to n, of e to the power negative lambda times lambda to the power x sub i, divided by x sub i factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.11)<\/strong> Ell of lambda equals negative n times lambda, plus the sum of x sub i times log lambda, minus the sum of log of x sub i factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.12)<\/strong> Ell prime of lambda equals negative n plus the sum of the observations divided by lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.13)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of lambda equals the sample mean X bar.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-04", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.4 · Poisson Process and Exponential Waiting Times", "Question": "What is a Poisson process, and how is it related to exponential waiting times?", "Short": "<p>A homogeneous Poisson process is a counting process that starts at zero, has independent increments, and has Poisson-distributed counts over each interval with mean equal to a constant rate times the interval length. Its inter-arrival waiting times are independent exponential variables with that rate.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A homogeneous Poisson process describes events accumulating over time at a constant rate r. The count starts at zero, counts in disjoint intervals are independent, and an interval count depends on the interval only through its length. The distribution of an increment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ N(t)-N(s)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(r(t-s)),\\qquad 0\\le s&lt;t. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus the expected number of events in an interval of length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(rh\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Keeping <span class=\"math inline\">\\(N(0)=0\\)<\/span> and stationary independent increments, an equivalent infinitesimal description is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(N(t+h)-N(t)=1)=rh+o(h),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(N(t+h)-N(t)\\ge2)=o(h),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The connection to exponential waiting times follows by considering the first event. If I call its waiting time T sub one, waiting longer than t means that no event has occurred by time t. Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(T_1&gt;t)=\\mathbb{P}(N(t)=0)=e^{-rt},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{T_1\\sim\\operatorname{Exponential}(r).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus Poisson describes <em>how many<\/em> events occur in an interval, whereas the exponential distribution describes <em>how long<\/em> one waits for the next event.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.5.1)<\/strong> N of t minus N of s follows a Poisson distribution with parameter r times the quantity t minus s, for zero less than or equal to s and s less than t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.2)<\/strong> The probability that the increment from time t to time t plus h equals one is r times h plus little-oh of h.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.3)<\/strong> Over a short interval of length h, the probability of one event is r times h plus little-oh of h, and the probability of two or more events is little-oh of h. Little-oh of h means a remainder that, divided by h, tends to zero as h tends to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.4)<\/strong> The probability that the first waiting time exceeds t equals the probability of zero events by time t, which is e to the power of negative r times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.5)<\/strong> T sub one follows an exponential distribution with rate r.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-05", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.5 · Exponential Distribution", "Question": "What is an exponential distribution?", "Short": "<p>An exponential distribution is a continuous distribution on nonnegative values with rate parameter lambda and density lambda times e raised to the power of negative lambda times t. It models the waiting time to the next event in a homogeneous Poisson process; its mean is one over lambda.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows an exponential distribution with positive rate lambda if it is nonnegative and has the following density:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_T(t)=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t},\\qquad t\\ge0. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Integrating this density from zero to t gives the CDF. Subtracting the CDF from one gives the survival probability, which is the probability of waiting longer than t. For nonnegative t, these are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_T(t)=1-e^{-\\lambda t}, \\qquad \\mathbb{P}(T&gt;t)=e^{-\\lambda t}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The mean and variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[T]=\\frac1\\lambda,} \\qquad \\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(T)=\\frac1{\\lambda^2}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The exponential distribution is memoryless. If no event has occurred during an initial waiting period, the distribution of the additional waiting time is the same as it was at the start. This property is expressed by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(T&gt;s+t\\mid T&gt;s)=\\mathbb{P}(T&gt;t). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here memorylessness holds for real <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s,t\\ge0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[T]=1/\\lambda\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\frac1{\\bar T}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate the rate from independent waiting times, I multiply the exponential densities:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\lambda)=\\prod_i\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t_i} =\\lambda^n e^{-\\lambda\\sum_i t_i}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Hence<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\lambda)=n\\log\\lambda-\\lambda\\sum_i t_i,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(\\lambda)=\\frac n\\lambda-\\sum_i t_i.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_i t_i&gt;0\\)<\/span> (which holds almost surely), setting this to zero yields<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac n{\\sum_i t_i} =\\frac1{\\bar T}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.9)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.6.1)<\/strong> Lowercase f sub T of t equals lambda times e raised to the power of negative lambda times t, for t greater than or equal to zero. The exponent is the negative of the product lambda times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.2)<\/strong> The CDF at t is one minus e to the power of negative lambda times t. The probability that T exceeds t is e to the power of negative lambda times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.3)<\/strong> The expected waiting time is one over lambda, and its variance is one over lambda squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.4)<\/strong> The probability that T exceeds s plus t, given that T exceeds s, equals the probability that T exceeds t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.5)<\/strong> Lambda hat equals one divided by T bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.6)<\/strong> The likelihood is the product of the exponential densities. It equals lambda to the power n times e raised to the negative of lambda times the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.7)<\/strong> Ell of lambda equals n times log lambda minus lambda times the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.8)<\/strong> Ell prime of lambda equals n over lambda minus the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.9)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of lambda is n divided by the sum of the observed waiting times, or the reciprocal of their sample mean.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-06", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.6 · Normal Distribution", "Question": "What is a normal distribution?", "Short": "<p>A normal distribution is a continuous distribution on the real line with a symmetric bell-shaped density, determined by its mean mu and positive variance sigma squared. Subtracting mu and dividing by sigma produces a standard normal variable with mean zero and variance one.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a normal distribution with mean mu and positive variance sigma squared if it has the following density on the real line:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_X(x)= \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left[-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right], \\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>We write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its mean and variance are exactly the parameters:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sigma^2.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The standard normal distribution is the special case with mean zero and variance one. I denote a standard normal variable by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\)<\/span>, then the standardized variable<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{Z=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an observed value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, the corresponding z-score is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>which measures how many standard deviations <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span> lies from the mean.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The CLT is a principal reason Gaussian sampling distributions are ubiquitous: sums and averages of many independent contributions often become approximately normal even when the individual observations are not normal.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mu, \\qquad \\mathbb{E}[X^2]=\\mu^2+\\sigma^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating with sample raw moments gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\mu_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X,}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{MoM}} =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i^2-\\bar X^2 =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To derive the maximum likelihood estimates of the mean and variance, I multiply the densities of the iid observations. The resulting likelihood is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\mu,\\sigma^2) =(2\\pi\\sigma^2)^{-n/2} \\exp\\left[-\\frac1{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2\\right].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\mu,\\sigma^2) =-\\frac n2\\log(2\\pi)-\\frac n2\\log\\sigma^2 -\\frac1{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I first differentiate the log-likelihood with respect to mu and set the derivative to zero:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\mu} =\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)=0,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\mu_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Substituting this and differentiating with respect to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2\\)<\/span> gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[-\\frac n{2\\sigma^2} +\\frac1{2\\sigma^4}\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar X)^2=0,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.14)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac1n\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.15)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a sample with positive residual sum of squares, this is the attained MLE. If all observations coincide, the likelihood is unbounded as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2\\downarrow0\\)<\/span> and there is no MLE in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>This MLE of variance is biased downward. The familiar unbiased sample variance is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ S^2=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.16)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This difference in denominators illustrates that maximum likelihood and unbiased estimation have different objectives: maximizing likelihood does not guarantee finite-sample unbiasedness.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.7.1)<\/strong> The density at x is one over the product of sigma and the square root of two pi, multiplied by an exponential. The exponent is the negative of the squared difference between x and mu, divided by two times sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.2)<\/strong> X is normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.3)<\/strong> The expected value of X equals mu, and its variance equals sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.4)<\/strong> Z follows a standard normal distribution: mean zero and variance one.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.5)<\/strong> Z equals X minus mu, all divided by sigma, and follows a standard normal distribution.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.6)<\/strong> The z-score is x minus mu, all divided by sigma.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.7)<\/strong> The expected value of X is mu, and the expected value of X squared is mu squared plus sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.8)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate of mu equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.9)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate of mu is X bar. The estimate of sigma squared is one over n times the sum of the squared deviations from X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.10)<\/strong> The likelihood equals the quantity two pi sigma squared raised to the power negative n over two, multiplied by an exponential. The exponent is negative one over two sigma squared times the sum of the squared deviations from mu.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.11)<\/strong> The log-likelihood is negative n over two times log of two pi, minus n over two times log of sigma squared, minus the sum of squared deviations from mu divided by two sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.12)<\/strong> The partial derivative of ell with respect to mu equals one over sigma squared times the sum of x sub i minus mu. I set that derivative equal to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.13)<\/strong> Mu hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.14)<\/strong> Negative n divided by two sigma squared, plus the sum of squared deviations from x bar divided by two sigma to the fourth power, equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.15)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of sigma squared is one over n times the sum, from i equals one to n, of the quantity x sub i minus x bar, squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.16)<\/strong> S squared is the sum, from i equals one to n, of the squared difference between X sub i and X bar, all divided by the quantity n minus one.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-07", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.7 · Uniform Distribution", "Question": "What is a uniform distribution?", "Short": "<p>A uniform distribution on an interval assigns equal probability to subintervals of equal length. Its density is constant at one divided by the interval length inside the interval and zero outside it.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable is uniformly distributed on the interval from a to b when its density is constant there and zero elsewhere. Assuming a is less than b, the density must integrate to one, so it is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_X(x)=\\frac1{b-a}\\mathbf 1_{[a,b]}(x). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>We write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(a,b).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Symmetry places its mean at the midpoint of the interval, and integration gives its variance. The resulting expressions are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2},} \\qquad \\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an estimation example, I consider independent observations uniformly distributed from zero to an unknown positive endpoint theta. The goal is to estimate that endpoint.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\theta/2\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=2\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To construct the likelihood, I must keep the support condition: any candidate endpoint smaller than an observed value makes the data impossible. Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\theta) =\\prod_{i=1}^n\\frac1\\theta\\mathbf 1_{[0,\\theta]}(x_i) =\\theta^{-n}\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\theta\\ge x_{(n)}\\}},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>where<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[x_{(n)}=\\max_i x_i.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\ge x_{(n)}\\)<\/span>, the factor <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta^{-n}\\)<\/span> decreases as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> grows. Therefore the maximum occurs at the smallest admissible value:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=X_{(n)}=\\max_iX_i.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This is an instructive example where MLE is obtained from the boundary of the parameter space rather than by setting a derivative to zero.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.8.1)<\/strong> The density is one divided by b minus a for x in the interval from a to b, and zero outside that interval.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.2)<\/strong> X is uniformly distributed on the interval from a to b.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.3)<\/strong> The mean is a plus b, all divided by two. The variance is the squared difference between b and a, divided by twelve.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.4)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate theta hat equals two times X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.5)<\/strong> The likelihood is the product of one over theta times the indicator that each observation lies between zero and theta. It equals theta to the power negative n if theta is at least the sample maximum, and zero otherwise.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.6)<\/strong> Lowercase x sub parenthesized n denotes the largest observed value, the sample maximum.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.7)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate theta hat equals the largest observation in the sample.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-08", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.8 · Student's t Distribution as a Reference Distribution", "Question": "What is Student’s t distribution?", "Short": "<p>Student’s t distribution is the distribution of a standard normal variable divided by the square root of an independent chi-squared variable divided by its degrees of freedom. It has heavier tails than a standard normal distribution and is the reference distribution for a standardized Gaussian sample mean when variance is estimated.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Student’s t distribution accounts for extra uncertainty introduced by estimating a variance. One construction starts with a standard normal variable Z and an independent chi-squared variable U with nu degrees of freedom:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1),\\qquad U\\sim\\chi^2_\\nu,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Using these independent variables, I form the ratio<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ T=\\frac{Z}{\\sqrt{U/\\nu}} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This ratio has Student’s t distribution with nu degrees of freedom, which I write as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T\\sim t_\\nu.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its density is symmetric around zero and has heavier tails than the standard normal. As <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\nu\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[t_\\nu\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This construction applies directly to an iid Gaussian sample with an unknown standard deviation. Using the sample standard deviation S in the denominator gives the pivot<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This pivot is the basis of the classical confidence interval and t-test for a Gaussian mean with unknown variance.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span> distribution is included here primarily as a reference distribution for inference, not as a core data-generating family whose parameter MLE is typically derived in a basic statistics course.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.9.1)<\/strong> Z is standard normal, and U follows a chi-squared distribution with nu degrees of freedom. Z and U are independent.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.2)<\/strong> T equals Z divided by the square root of the quantity U divided by nu.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.3)<\/strong> T follows Student’s t distribution with nu degrees of freedom.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.4)<\/strong> As nu tends to infinity, the t distribution approaches the standard normal distribution.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.5)<\/strong> X bar minus mu, divided by the quantity S over the square root of n, follows a t distribution with n minus one degrees of freedom.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-09", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.9 · Distribution Relationships to Remember", "Question": "What relationships connect the main probability distributions?", "Short": "<p>The distribution relationships connect binary trials, counts, waiting times, and sampling distributions. A binomial variable sums Bernoulli trials, a geometric variable counts trials to the first success, and a Poisson count arises in a rare-event limit. Exponential waiting times belong to a Poisson process, while normal and t distributions describe important standardized quantities.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>For a quick comparison, I summarize the construction behind each relationship in the following table.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Relationship<\/strong><\/th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bernoulli <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Binomial<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A binomial count is the sum of iid Bernoulli trials.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Binomial <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Poisson<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rare-event limit: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_n\\to0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(np_n\\to\\lambda\\)<\/span>.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bernoulli <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Geometric<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Geometric is the number of Bernoulli trials until the first success.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Poisson process <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Exponential<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Poisson counts events; exponential describes inter-arrival waiting times.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arbitrary iid data <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Gaussian average<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Under finite variance, the CLT gives an asymptotically normal standardized sum/mean.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Continuous <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Uniform<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_X(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)\\)<\/span> under the probability integral transform.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Normal sample, unknown <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standardizing the sample mean by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)<\/span> produces a Student <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span> statistic.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>\n", "Readings": ""}]}, "04": {"title": "Point Estimation", "cards": [{"ID": "s4-01", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.1 · Parameter", "Question": "What is a parameter?", "Short": "<p>A parameter is a fixed but usually unknown quantity that determines a distribution within a statistical model.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A parameter specifies which distribution in a model describes the population. In frequentist statistics, I treat its true value as fixed even though I do not know it. I use theta as a general symbol; in a Bernoulli model it can be the success probability p, in a Poisson model the mean count lambda, and in a normal model the pair consisting of the mean and variance.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s4-02", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.2 · Statistic", "Question": "What is a statistic?", "Short": "<p>A statistic is a measurable function of the sample that does not depend on unknown parameters.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A statistic, by contrast, is calculated from the sample. It must not contain an unknown parameter, so its value can be computed from the observations alone. Formally, I write it as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T=T(X_1,\\dots,X_n).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.1.1)<\/strong> T is a function of the sample X sub one through X sub n. Theta hat is a statistic used to estimate theta.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-03", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.3 · Estimator", "Question": "What is an estimator?", "Short": "<p>An estimator is a statistic used to estimate a parameter.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>An estimator is a statistic <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat\\theta=T(X_1,\\dots,X_n)\\)<\/span> used to estimate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>. Before data are observed, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat\\theta\\)<\/span> is itself a random variable.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s4-04", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.4 · Estimate", "Question": "What is an estimate?", "Short": "<p>An estimate is the numerical value of an estimator for the observed sample.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>After observing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)<\/span>, the realized numerical value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat\\theta(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\)<\/span> is called an <em>estimate<\/em>.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s4-05", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.5 · Unbiasedness", "Question": "What does it mean for an estimator to be unbiased?", "Short": "<p>An estimator is unbiased if its expected value equals the true parameter for every admissible parameter value. This is a statement about its average over repeated samples; it does not mean that any particular estimate equals the truth.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Unbiasedness describes the average behavior of an estimator under repeated sampling. I first define bias as the difference between its expected value and the parameter being estimated:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\operatorname{Bias}_\\theta(\\hat\\theta) =\\mathbb{E}_\\theta[\\hat\\theta]-\\theta. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Setting this difference to zero gives the condition for an unbiased estimator:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}_\\theta[\\hat\\theta]=\\theta}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>for every admissible <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_1,\\dots,X_n\\)<\/span> are iid with mean <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu\\)<\/span>, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[\\bar X]=\\frac1n\\sum_i\\mathbb{E}[X_i]=\\mu,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bar X\\)<\/span> is an unbiased estimator of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example-1\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>For Gaussian data, the MLE<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\frac1n\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>is biased. The correction<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S^2=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>is unbiased for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2\\)<\/span> for any iid sample with finite variance and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\ge2\\)<\/span>; Gaussianity is not required.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.2.1)<\/strong> The bias of theta hat is the expected value of theta hat minus the true parameter theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.2.2)<\/strong> Theta hat is unbiased if its expected value equals theta for every admissible value of theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.2.3)<\/strong> The expected value of X bar equals one over n times the sum of the individual expected values, which equals mu.<\/p><p><strong>(4.2.4)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of sigma squared equals the sum of squared deviations from X bar divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(4.2.5)<\/strong> S squared equals the sum of squared deviations from X bar divided by the quantity n minus one.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-06", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.6 · Consistency", "Question": "What does it mean for an estimator to be consistent?", "Short": "<p>An estimator is consistent if it converges in probability to the true parameter as the sample size tends to infinity. Equivalently, the probability that its estimation error exceeds any fixed positive tolerance tends to zero.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Consistency describes how estimation improves as the sample grows. A sequence of estimators is consistent for theta when it converges in probability to theta, which I write as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\theta_n\\xrightarrow{P}\\theta.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>To unpack convergence in probability, I choose any fixed positive tolerance epsilon. Consistency requires that the chance of exceeding that tolerance eventually becomes arbitrarily small:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}_\\theta(|\\hat\\theta_n-\\theta|&gt;\\varepsilon)\\to0 \\quad\\text{as }n\\to\\infty. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"intuition\" class=\"unnumbered\">Intuition<\/h3>\n<p>Unbiasedness is a finite-sample statement about the estimator’s mean. Consistency is an asymptotic statement saying that with enough data the estimator concentrates around the true parameter.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>An estimator can be biased but consistent. For example, the Gaussian MLE of variance with denominator <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> is biased for finite <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> but consistent as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.3.1)<\/strong> Theta hat sub n converges in probability to theta as n tends to infinity.<\/p><p><strong>(4.3.2)<\/strong> For every positive epsilon, the probability that the absolute difference between theta hat sub n and theta exceeds epsilon tends to zero as n tends to infinity.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-07", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.7 · Statistical Bias vs. Machine-Learning Bias", "Question": "How does statistical bias differ from machine-learning bias?", "Short": "<p>Statistical estimator bias and machine-learning prediction bias both describe systematic error under repeated sampling. Estimator bias compares an expected parameter estimate with the true parameter; prediction bias compares the expected fitted prediction at an input with the true regression function at that input.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Statistical bias concerns the quantity being estimated. For a scalar parameter, I compare the expected estimator with that parameter:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Bias}(\\hat\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}[\\hat\\theta]-\\theta.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For prediction under squared-error loss, the target is the true conditional mean function f of x. I imagine fitting the model on repeated random training sets D and averaging its predictions at the same input x. The pointwise prediction bias is then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Bias}(x) =\\mathbb{E}_D[\\hat f_D(x)]-f(x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The concepts share the idea of systematic error but refer to different objects.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.5.1)<\/strong> The bias of theta hat is the expected value of theta hat minus the true parameter theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.5.2)<\/strong> The bias at x is the expected prediction f hat sub D of x, averaged over random training sets D, minus the true regression function f of x.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-08", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.8 · Method of Moments", "Question": "What is the method of moments?", "Short": "<p>The method of moments estimates model parameters by equating theoretical moments to sample moments and solving the resulting equations. For example, when a distribution’s mean equals its parameter, the first-moment estimate is the sample mean.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The method of moments links quantities I can calculate from data to corresponding quantities implied by a model. Suppose the model has k unknown parameters collected in theta, and its required theoretical moments exist. I express those moments as functions of theta:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[m_j(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}_\\theta[X^j].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The corresponding sample moments are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat m_j=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i^j.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I then equate the theoretical and sample moments and solve the system<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat m_j=m_j(\\theta),\\qquad j=1,\\dots,k, }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>for the unknown parameters. The chosen moments must identify the parameters, and the equations need a solution in the allowed parameter space.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>For a Bernoulli distribution, the expected value equals p, so matching the first moment gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat p=\\bar X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The same logic applies to a Poisson distribution: its expected value is lambda, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat\\lambda=\\bar X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For an exponential distribution parameterized by its rate, the mean is the reciprocal of lambda. Matching it gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat\\lambda=1/\\bar X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For a normal distribution with both parameters unknown, I need two moments. Using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mu\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X^2]=\\mu^2+\\sigma^2\\)<\/span> gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat\\mu=\\bar X, \\qquad \\hat\\sigma^2=\\frac1n\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.6.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.6.1)<\/strong> The j-th theoretical raw moment, m sub j of theta, is the expected value under theta of X to the power of j.<\/p><p><strong>(4.6.2)<\/strong> The j-th sample moment is one over n times the sum, from i equals one to n, of X sub i to the power of j.<\/p><p><strong>(4.6.3)<\/strong> Set each sample moment m hat sub j equal to the corresponding theoretical moment m sub j of theta, for j from one to k, and solve for theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.6.4)<\/strong> Mu hat equals X bar, and sigma squared hat equals the sum of squared deviations from X bar divided by n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-09", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.9 · Maximum Likelihood Estimation", "Question": "What is maximum likelihood estimation?", "Short": "<p>Maximum likelihood estimation chooses the parameter value that maximizes the likelihood of the observed sample. The observations are held fixed while the parameter varies. For independent observations, the likelihood is the product of their probability masses or densities; maximizing its logarithm gives the same result.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Maximum likelihood estimation asks which parameter value makes the observed sample have the largest probability mass or density under the specified model. For iid observations with probability function f, I form the likelihood by multiplication:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ L(\\theta)=\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\theta), }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here theta varies and the observations stay fixed. The likelihood is not a probability distribution over theta. Taking its logarithm turns the product into a sum:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\theta)=\\log L(\\theta)=\\sum_i\\log f(x_i;\\theta).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Because the logarithm is strictly increasing, maximizing the likelihood or the log-likelihood gives the same estimator. I express that optimization as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\arg\\max_\\theta L(\\theta) =\\arg\\max_\\theta\\ell(\\theta). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.7.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The distribution chapters contain full MLE derivations for Bernoulli, Binomial, Geometric, Poisson, Exponential, Normal, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Uniform}(0,\\theta)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"asymptotic-behavior.\">Asymptotic behavior.<\/h4>\n<p>Under appropriate regularity and identifiability conditions and correct model specification, an MLE is consistent and asymptotically normal. In the scalar case, its limiting behavior is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\sqrt n(\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MLE}}-\\theta) \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}\\left(0,I(\\theta)^{-1}\\right),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.7.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I(\\theta)\\)<\/span> is Fisher information per observation. This result motivates many large-sample confidence intervals and tests.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.7.1)<\/strong> L of theta equals the product, from i equals one to n, of f of x sub i evaluated at parameter theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.7.2)<\/strong> The log-likelihood, ell of theta, equals the log of L of theta, or the sum of the individual log densities or log probability masses.<\/p><p><strong>(4.7.3)<\/strong> Theta hat, the maximum likelihood estimator, is the value of theta that maximizes the likelihood. It also maximizes the log-likelihood.<\/p><p><strong>(4.7.4)<\/strong> Under regularity conditions, the square root of n times the estimation error theta hat minus theta converges in distribution to a normal random variable with mean zero and variance equal to the inverse of the Fisher information per observation.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-10", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.10 · MAP Estimation and the Bayesian View", "Question": "What is MAP estimation?", "Short": "<p>Maximum a posteriori estimation chooses the parameter value with the highest posterior density. It combines the likelihood with a prior, whereas maximum likelihood uses only the likelihood. Equivalently, MAP maximizes the sum of the log-likelihood and the log-prior.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Maximum a posteriori estimation starts from the posterior distribution of a parameter. In Bayesian inference, a prior describes uncertainty about theta before observing the data; the likelihood describes how the data depend on theta. Bayes’ theorem combines them:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p(\\theta\\mid D) =\\frac{p(D\\mid\\theta)p(\\theta)}{p(D)}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The denominator does not depend on theta, so finding the mode of the posterior is equivalent to maximizing the product of the likelihood and prior:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MAP}} =\\arg\\max_\\theta p(\\theta\\mid D) =\\arg\\max_\\theta p(D\\mid\\theta)p(\\theta). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equivalently,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MAP}} =\\arg\\max_\\theta \\left[\\log p(D\\mid\\theta)+\\log p(\\theta)\\right].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>MLE uses the likelihood only; MAP combines likelihood and prior information.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"connection-to-regularization.\">Connection to regularization.<\/h4>\n<p>The prior also connects MAP to regularization. For example, a Gaussian prior<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[w\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\tau^2I)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>has log-density equal, up to a constant, to<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[-\\frac1{2\\tau^2}\\|w\\|_2^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore MAP estimation corresponds to an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L_2\\)<\/span> penalty. A Laplace prior<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p(w)\\propto e^{-\\lambda|w|}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.9.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>corresponds to an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L_1\\)<\/span> penalty.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.9.1)<\/strong> The posterior density of theta given D equals the likelihood of D given theta times the prior density of theta, all divided by the marginal probability or density of D.<\/p><p><strong>(4.9.2)<\/strong> Theta hat, the maximum a posteriori estimator, is the value of theta that maximizes the posterior density, or equivalently the likelihood times the prior density.<\/p><p><strong>(4.9.3)<\/strong> Maximize the sum of the log-likelihood and the log-prior over theta.<\/p><p><strong>(4.9.4)<\/strong> W follows a multivariate normal distribution with mean vector zero and covariance matrix tau squared times the identity matrix.<\/p><p><strong>(4.9.5)<\/strong> Negative one over two tau squared, times the squared L-two norm of w.<\/p><p><strong>(4.9.6)<\/strong> The prior density of w is proportional to e to the power of negative lambda times the absolute value of w.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s4-11", "Deck": "04", "Topic": "4.11 · Selection Bias", "Question": "What is selection bias?", "Short": "<p>Selection bias occurs when the way observations enter a sample makes an unadjusted analysis systematically misrepresent the target population or target effect. A larger sample from the same biased selection process does not by itself remove that bias.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Selection bias is a mismatch introduced by how data are selected. I use S equal to one to indicate that an observation is included. If inclusion depends on the outcome or related variables, the observed distribution may differ from the population distribution. One warning sign is that the inclusion probability<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(S=1\\mid X,Y)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.10.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>varies with relevant variables. This can produce the distributional mismatch<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(Y\\mid S=1)\\ne\\mathbb{P}(Y). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(4.10.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Estimating population income using only premium-banking customers is biased if the probability of being sampled increases with income. Increasing the sample size within the same biased selection mechanism does not fix the problem.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(4.10.1)<\/strong> The probability of inclusion in the sample, given X and Y.<\/p><p><strong>(4.10.2)<\/strong> The distribution of Y conditional on being selected differs from the distribution of Y in the target population.<\/p>"}]}, "05": {"title": "Confidence Intervals", "cards": [{"ID": "s5-01", "Deck": "05", "Topic": "5.1 · Frequentist Definition", "Question": "What is a frequentist confidence interval, and how should it be interpreted?", "Short": "<p>A confidence interval is an interval calculated from a sample by a procedure with a specified coverage probability. For a 95 percent confidence procedure, at least 95 percent of the intervals it produces over repeated samples cover the true parameter, under the model assumptions.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A confidence interval describes uncertainty about a fixed parameter using limits calculated from random data. I denote the lower and upper limits by L of X and U of X, so the interval is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[C(X)=[L(X),U(X)]\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A confidence level of one minus alpha means that the procedure is constructed to satisfy<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}_\\theta(\\theta\\in C(X))\\ge1-\\alpha }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>for every parameter value in the intended model. The probability refers to repeated sampling, which changes the interval, while the true parameter stays fixed.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>After observing a particular frequentist interval, I do not say that the fixed parameter has a 95 percent probability of being inside it. The 95 percent refers to the coverage of the procedure. Exactly calibrated intervals have exactly nominal coverage, conservative intervals have at least nominal coverage, and approximate intervals achieve their nominal coverage only approximately under appropriate assumptions.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(5.1.1)<\/strong> C of X is the interval from L of X to U of X.<\/p><p><strong>(5.1.2)<\/strong> For every parameter value theta, the probability that the random interval C of X contains theta is at least one minus alpha.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s5-02", "Deck": "05", "Topic": "5.2 · Normal Mean with Known Variance: z Interval", "Question": "What is the confidence interval for a normal mean with known variance?", "Short": "<p>A z confidence interval for a normal population mean with known variance is the sample mean plus or minus a standard normal critical value times the known standard error. It follows by standardizing the sample mean and using the central probability of the standard normal distribution.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A z interval uses a standard normal reference distribution to quantify uncertainty about a mean. For an exact interval, I assume an iid normal sample:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_1,\\dots,X_n\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I assume sigma is known. Under this model, the sample mean has distribution<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\bar X\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Subtracting the population mean and dividing by the standard error produces a pivot, a quantity with a known distribution that does not depend on the unknown mean:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z=\\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I choose the standard normal quantile at one minus alpha over two so that the central interval contains probability one minus alpha:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(-z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\le Z\\le z_{1-\\alpha/2})=1-\\alpha.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Solving the inequality for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu\\)<\/span> yields<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mu\\in \\bar X\\pm z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For 95% confidence, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z_{0.975}\\approx1.96\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(5.2.1)<\/strong> X sub one through X sub n are independent and identically distributed normal variables with mean mu and variance sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(5.2.2)<\/strong> X bar is normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(5.2.3)<\/strong> Z equals X bar minus mu, divided by sigma over the square root of n. Z follows a standard normal distribution.<\/p><p><strong>(5.2.4)<\/strong> The probability that Z lies between minus z sub one minus alpha over two and plus z sub one minus alpha over two equals one minus alpha.<\/p><p><strong>(5.2.5)<\/strong> The confidence interval is X bar plus or minus the standard normal quantile at one minus alpha over two, times sigma divided by the square root of n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s5-03", "Deck": "05", "Topic": "5.3 · Normal Mean with Unknown Variance: t Interval", "Question": "What is the confidence interval for a normal mean with unknown variance?", "Short": "<p>A t confidence interval for a normal population mean with unknown variance is the sample mean plus or minus a t critical value times the estimated standard error. The reference distribution has n minus one degrees of freedom because the variance is estimated from the same sample.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A t interval accounts for estimating the standard deviation rather than knowing it. For an iid Gaussian sample with n at least two, I replace sigma by the sample standard deviation S. The resulting standardized mean has the exact distribution<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Using its central probability of one minus alpha and solving for mu gives the interval<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mu\\in \\bar X\\pm t_{1-\\alpha/2,n-1}\\frac{S}{\\sqrt n}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The heavier tails of the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span> distribution account for uncertainty from estimating <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(5.3.1)<\/strong> X bar minus mu, divided by S over the square root of n, follows a t distribution with n minus one degrees of freedom.<\/p><p><strong>(5.3.2)<\/strong> The confidence interval is X bar plus or minus the t quantile at one minus alpha over two, with n minus one degrees of freedom, times S divided by the square root of n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s5-04", "Deck": "05", "Topic": "5.4 · Confidence Interval for a Proportion", "Question": "How do you construct a confidence interval for a conversion rate?", "Short": "<p>A confidence interval for a proportion estimates an unknown population probability from binary observations. A simple large-sample Wald interval is the observed proportion plus or minus a standard normal critical value times its estimated standard error. Its accuracy can be poor with small samples or proportions near zero or one.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A confidence interval for a proportion starts from the sampling distribution of the observed success rate. With iid Bernoulli observations and common success probability p, the estimator and its variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat p=\\bar X, \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)=\\frac{p(1-p)}n.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.4.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For sufficiently large samples and a probability away from the boundaries, the central limit theorem motivates the approximation<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat p\\approx\\mathcal{N}\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}n\\right).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.4.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Replacing the unknown probability inside the standard error by the observed proportion gives the Wald interval:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat p\\pm z_{1-\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}n}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.4.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The Wald interval can behave poorly for small samples or probabilities near <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span>; Wilson or exact methods are often preferable. For basic large-sample inference about conversion rates, the normal approximation is usually the key conceptual tool.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(5.4.1)<\/strong> P hat equals X bar. Its variance is p times one minus p, divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(5.4.2)<\/strong> P hat is approximately normally distributed with mean p and variance p times one minus p, divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(5.4.3)<\/strong> The Wald interval is p hat plus or minus the standard normal quantile at one minus alpha over two, times the square root of p hat times one minus p hat, divided by n. The entire variance expression is inside the square root.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s5-05", "Deck": "05", "Topic": "5.5 · Confidence Intervals and Hypothesis Tests", "Question": "How can a confidence interval be used to test a null hypothesis?", "Short": "<p>A confidence interval and a hypothesis test give matching decisions when the interval is obtained by inverting that same family of tests. A candidate parameter value is excluded from the interval exactly when its null hypothesis is rejected, with matching significance levels and boundary conventions.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The connection between a confidence interval and a test comes from collecting all parameter values that would not be rejected. Consider the two-sided level-alpha test<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\theta=\\theta_0,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For every candidate theta sub zero, I test that null and retain it if the test does not reject. The retained values form the corresponding confidence set at level one minus alpha. With matching boundary conventions, the relationship is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\theta_0\\notin CI_{1-\\alpha} \\iff \\text{reject }H_0. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For example, excluding zero from the matching confidence interval for a difference can be written as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[0\\notin CI_{95\\%}(\\Delta)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(5.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>corresponds to statistical significance at the 5% level for the matching two-sided test of zero effect. A pooled two-proportion z-test and an unpooled Wald interval use different standard errors, so their decisions need not coincide exactly near the threshold.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(5.5.1)<\/strong> H naught states that theta equals theta sub zero.<\/p><p><strong>(5.5.2)<\/strong> Reject H naught if and only if theta sub zero lies outside the corresponding confidence interval.<\/p><p><strong>(5.5.3)<\/strong> Zero is not contained in the ninety-five percent confidence interval for delta.<\/p>"}]}, "06": {"title": "Hypothesis Testing", "cards": [{"ID": "s6-01", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.1 · Null and Alternative Hypotheses", "Question": "What are the null and alternative hypotheses?", "Short": "<p>The null hypothesis is a statement about population parameters that we test against an alternative. The alternative describes the competing parameter values. For example, the null may state that two conversion probabilities are equal, while the alternative states that they differ.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The null and alternative hypotheses specify competing claims about a population or a statistical model. I represent them by disjoint sets of possible parameter values. If capital Theta is the full parameter space, I write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\theta\\in\\Theta_0, \\qquad H_1:\\theta\\in\\Theta_1,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>with disjoint parameter sets <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Theta_0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Theta_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>A hypothesis is simple when it specifies the entire data distribution, and composite when it allows more than one distribution. For example, with a normal model and known variance, the statement<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\mu=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>is simple because the mean and variance are both specified. If the variance is unknown, the same statement leaves a nuisance parameter unspecified and is composite. More generally, a composite hypothesis permits multiple distributions, for example<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\mu\\le0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.1.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The null hypothesis does not universally mean “the target is absent.” It is simply the baseline statistical claim being tested: equality of two rates, a coefficient equal to zero, a parameter equal to a benchmark, and so on.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>In a conversion experiment, I define delta as the treatment conversion probability minus the control conversion probability. To test for any difference, I use the two-sided hypotheses<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\Delta=0, \\qquad H_1:\\Delta\\ne0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.1.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If only an increase is scientifically relevant and the direction is specified in advance, one may use<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\Delta\\le0, \\qquad H_1:\\Delta&gt;0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.1.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.1.1)<\/strong> H naught states that theta belongs to capital Theta sub zero. H one states that theta belongs to capital Theta sub one.<\/p><p><strong>(6.1.2)<\/strong> H naught: mu equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(6.1.3)<\/strong> H naught: mu is less than or equal to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(6.1.4)<\/strong> For a two-sided test, H naught states that delta equals zero; H one states that delta is not equal to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(6.1.5)<\/strong> For an improvement test, H naught states that delta is less than or equal to zero; H one states that delta is greater than zero.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-02", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.2 · Test Statistic and Reference Distribution", "Question": "What is a test statistic, and what is its null distribution?", "Short": "<p>A test statistic is a quantity calculated from the sample whose extreme values provide evidence against the null hypothesis. Its reference distribution describes how that quantity behaves under the null, allowing us to judge whether the observed result is unusual.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A test statistic summarizes the part of the sample relevant to a hypothesis test. Like any statistic, it is a function of the observations:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T=T(X_1,\\dots,X_n)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>To interpret its value, I compare it with its null reference distribution, known exactly or approximately. Values judged extreme by the chosen test provide evidence against the null. Compatibility with the null means that the result is not unusually extreme under this comparison; it does not prove that the null is true.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>For a concrete example, I take an iid normal sample:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_i\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>with known <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span>, and test<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Under <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ Z=\\frac{\\bar X-\\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The standard normal is therefore the reference distribution under <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H_0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.2.1)<\/strong> The test statistic T is a function of X sub one through X sub n.<\/p><p><strong>(6.2.2)<\/strong> The X sub i are independent and identically distributed normal variables with mean mu and variance sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(6.2.3)<\/strong> H naught states that mu equals mu sub zero.<\/p><p><strong>(6.2.4)<\/strong> Under H naught, Z equals X bar minus mu sub zero, divided by sigma over the square root of n, and follows a standard normal distribution.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-03", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.3 · Critical Region and Significance Level", "Question": "What are the critical region and significance level?", "Short": "<p>A rejection region is the set of test-statistic values for which we reject the null hypothesis. The significance level alpha is an upper bound on the probability of rejecting a true null. We choose the region so that this error probability is controlled under the null model.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A rejection region turns a test statistic into a decision rule: I reject the null when the statistic falls in that region. I choose the region before interpreting the observed result, using a significance level alpha to bound the chance of a false rejection. For a simple null, the condition is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}_{H_0}(T\\in R) =\\mathbb{P}(\\text{reject }H_0\\mid H_0\\text{ true}) \\le\\alpha. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The rejection probability is the actual Type I error rate; <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> is its upper bound. Equality holds for an exactly calibrated test, but need not hold for a conservative or discrete test.<\/p>\n<p>For a composite null, I need error control at every parameter value allowed by the null. Equivalently, even the largest null rejection probability must satisfy<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0} \\mathbb{P}_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0) \\le\\alpha.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>For a one-sided z-test with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H_1:\\mu&gt;\\mu_0\\)<\/span>, reject if<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z&gt;z_{1-\\alpha}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.3.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>At <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=0.05\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z_{0.95}\\approx1.645\\)<\/span>. For a two-sided test, reject if<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[|Z|&gt;z_{1-\\alpha/2},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.3.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>which at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=0.05\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(|Z|&gt;1.96\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.3.1)<\/strong> Under the null, the probability that T falls in the rejection region R is the probability of rejecting H naught, and is at most alpha.<\/p><p><strong>(6.3.2)<\/strong> The supremum, over all theta in the null parameter space, of the probability of rejecting H naught is at most alpha.<\/p><p><strong>(6.3.3)<\/strong> Reject when Z is greater than the standard normal quantile at one minus alpha.<\/p><p><strong>(6.3.4)<\/strong> Reject when the absolute value of Z exceeds the standard normal quantile at one minus alpha over two.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-04", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.4 · p-value as a Statistic and Random Variable", "Question": "What is a p-value?", "Short": "<p>A p-value is the probability, assuming the null hypothesis is true, of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the observed one. Extremeness is defined by the chosen test. A small p-value indicates incompatibility with the null model; it is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A p-value measures how unusual the observed statistic would be if the null model were true. I compare the observed value with all values at least as extreme according to the chosen test. For a one-sided test in which larger values count as more extreme, and with a fully specified null reference distribution, this tail probability is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ p_{\\mathrm{obs}} =\\mathbb{P}_{H_0}(T\\ge T_{\\mathrm{obs}}). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.4.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"follow-up-a-composite-null.\">Follow-up: a composite null.<\/h4>\n<p>If the null permits several parameter values, there may be several null tail probabilities. One valid construction takes their supremum, so that validity holds across the null:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p_{\\mathrm{obs}}=\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0}\\mathbb{P}_\\theta(T\\ge T_{\\mathrm{obs}}).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.4.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a one-sided normal mean test with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H_0:\\mu\\le\\mu_0\\)<\/span>, the supremum is at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu_0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Before the data are observed, the p-value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=p(X)\\)<\/span> is itself a random variable because it is a function of the random sample.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"two-sided-example.\">Two-sided example.<\/h4>\n<p>For a two-sided z-test, extreme values can occur in either tail. Symmetry lets me add the two equal tail areas, giving<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ p=2\\left[1-\\Phi(|z_{\\mathrm{obs}}|)\\right]. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.4.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"follow-up-the-distribution-of-the-p-value.\">Follow-up: the distribution of the p-value.<\/h4>\n<p>The distribution of p-values under repeated sampling is a separate property from their definition. When I compute an exact tail probability using a continuous, fully specified null reference distribution, the p-value is uniform under that reference null:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{p\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.4.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a one-sided test, this follows from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=1-F_0(T)\\)<\/span> and the probability integral transform. Exact means that I use the true null distribution rather than a large-sample approximation; it does not refer to decimal precision. More generally, a valid p-value need only satisfy<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}_\\theta(p\\le u)\\le u, \\qquad 0\\le u\\le1,\\quad\\theta\\in\\Theta_0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.4.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>For discrete tests or parameter values inside a composite null, p-values may be conservative rather than uniform. For example, a one-sided z-test calibrated at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu_0\\)<\/span> is conservative when the true mean is below <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mu_0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.4.1)<\/strong> The observed p-value is the probability, under the null hypothesis, that T is at least as large as its observed value.<\/p><p><strong>(6.4.2)<\/strong> For this composite-null construction, take the supremum of that tail probability over all parameter values allowed by H naught.<\/p><p><strong>(6.4.3)<\/strong> The two-sided p-value equals two times the quantity one minus capital Phi evaluated at the absolute value of the observed z-score. Capital Phi is the standard normal CDF.<\/p><p><strong>(6.4.4)<\/strong> Under the stated exact continuous reference null, the p-value follows a uniform distribution between zero and one.<\/p><p><strong>(6.4.5)<\/strong> For every theta in the null parameter space and every u between zero and one, the probability that the p-value is at most u is no greater than u.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-05", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.5 · Type I Error", "Question": "What is a Type I error?", "Short": "<p>A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A Type I error is a false rejection: the null is true, but the procedure rejects it. A level-alpha test controls its probability by requiring<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0)\\le\\alpha, \\qquad\\theta\\in\\Theta_0. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.5.1)<\/strong> For every theta in the null parameter space, the probability of rejecting H naught is at most alpha.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-06", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.6 · Type II Error", "Question": "What is a Type II error?", "Short": "<p>A Type II error is failing to reject the null when a specified alternative is true.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A Type II error is a failure to detect a departure from the null. Its probability depends on which alternative value is true, because small effects are generally harder to detect than large effects. For a specified alternative theta, I define<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\beta(\\theta)= \\mathbb{P}_\\theta(\\text{do not reject }H_0), \\qquad \\theta\\in\\Theta_1. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.5.2)<\/strong> Beta of theta is the probability of failing to reject H naught when theta belongs to the alternative parameter space.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-07", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.7 · Statistical Power", "Question": "What is statistical power?", "Short": "<p>Statistical power is the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when a specified alternative is true; it equals one minus the Type II error probability.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Power is the complementary probability of detecting that alternative. I obtain the power function by subtracting the Type II error probability from one:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\pi(\\theta) =\\mathbb{P}_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0) =1-\\beta(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta\\in\\Theta_1. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Power is therefore the probability of detecting a real effect of a specified size.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.5.3)<\/strong> Power at theta, pi of theta, is the probability of rejecting H naught at that alternative. It equals one minus beta of theta.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-08", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.8 · Statistical vs. Practical Significance", "Question": "What is the difference between statistical and practical significance?", "Short": "<p>Statistical significance means the result meets a pre-specified criterion for incompatibility with the null model. Practical significance means the effect is large enough to matter for the decision. An effect can be statistically significant but too small to justify acting on it.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Statistical significance and practical significance answer different questions. A test of zero effect can reject because an estimate is precise, even when the effect is tiny. To decide whether the change matters, I also consider its magnitude, confidence interval, costs, and a business threshold.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>With an enormous sample, a change from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(10.000\\%\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(10.001\\%\\)<\/span> can have a tiny p-value but negligible business value. Decisions should therefore use the estimated effect, confidence interval, and business threshold rather than p-value alone.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s6-09", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.9 · One-Sided vs. Two-Sided Tests", "Question": "When should you use a one-sided or a two-sided test?", "Short": "<p>A two-sided test looks for departures from the null in either direction. A one-sided test looks for a departure in a pre-specified direction and can have more power in that direction. I choose the direction before examining the results, rather than selecting it to fit the observed effect.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A two-sided test is appropriate when either an increase or a decrease matters. For a scalar parameter, I express its alternative as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_1:\\theta\\ne\\theta_0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.9.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a standard equal-tailed test, I allocate alpha over two to each tail. If the question concerns only a pre-specified increase, I instead use the one-sided alternative<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_1:\\theta&gt;\\theta_0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This puts the Type I error budget into the relevant tail and lowers the critical threshold in that direction compared with the corresponding two-sided test. That is the source of the power advantage. Choosing a direction after seeing the data changes the testing procedure and invalidates the claimed calibration.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.9.1)<\/strong> H one: theta is not equal to theta sub zero.<\/p><p><strong>(6.9.2)<\/strong> H one: theta is greater than theta sub zero.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s6-10", "Deck": "06", "Topic": "6.10 · Multiple Testing", "Question": "What is the multiple-testing problem?", "Short": "<p>Multiple testing means making several inferential comparisons, which creates more opportunities for false discoveries. Family-wise error rate controls the probability of any false rejection, while false discovery rate controls the expected proportion of false rejections among discoveries. The correction should match which error rate the decision requires.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Multiple testing matters because even individually calibrated tests can produce a large probability of at least one false discovery. If m independent true nulls are each tested with actual Type I error probability alpha, that family-wise probability is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[1-(1-\\alpha)^m.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m=20\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=0.05\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[1-0.95^{20}\\approx0.642.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus naive repetition of 5% tests can create many false discoveries.<\/p>\n<p>The family-wise error rate, or FWER, formalizes the risk of making any false rejection in the family:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(\\text{at least one false rejection in the family}).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Bonferroni controls that risk without requiring independence. I divide the overall alpha budget among the m tests and use the threshold<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{individual}}=\\frac\\alpha m.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The reason this works is the union bound: the probability of any false rejection is at most the sum of the individual false-rejection probabilities. Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}\\left(\\bigcup_{j=1}^m FP_j\\right) \\le\\sum_{j=1}^m\\mathbb{P}(FP_j) \\le m\\frac\\alpha m=\\alpha.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Holm’s step-down method also controls FWER and is generally less conservative than plain Bonferroni.<\/p>\n<p>False discovery rate, or FDR, asks a different question: what fraction of the reported discoveries are false, on average? If V counts false discoveries and R counts all discoveries, I define<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\operatorname{FDR} =\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\frac{V}{\\max(R,1)}\\right]. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(6.10.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The Benjamini–Hochberg procedure controls FDR under independence and certain forms of positive dependence (PRDS). It does not guarantee control under arbitrary dependence; Benjamini–Yekutieli is a more conservative option for that setting.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(6.10.1)<\/strong> For m independent tests at exact size alpha, the probability of at least one false positive is one minus the quantity one minus alpha raised to the power of m.<\/p><p><strong>(6.10.2)<\/strong> One minus zero point nine five to the twentieth power is approximately zero point six four two.<\/p><p><strong>(6.10.3)<\/strong> The family-wise error rate is the probability of at least one false rejection in the family of tests.<\/p><p><strong>(6.10.4)<\/strong> The significance threshold for each individual test is alpha divided by m.<\/p><p><strong>(6.10.5)<\/strong> The probability of the union of the false-positive events is at most the sum of their probabilities. Each is at most alpha divided by m, so the sum is at most alpha.<\/p><p><strong>(6.10.6)<\/strong> The false discovery rate is the expected value of V divided by the maximum of R and one. V is the number of false discoveries and R is the total number of discoveries.<\/p>"}]}, "07": {"title": "A/B Testing", "cards": [{"ID": "s7-01", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.1 · What an A/B Test Is", "Question": "What is an A/B test?", "Short": "<p>An A/B test is a randomized controlled experiment comparing a control version with a treatment version. Random assignment lets us estimate the causal effect of the change under the experiment’s assumptions.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>An A/B test evaluates a change by assigning eligible experimental units at random to a control or treatment arm. The control receives the baseline version, while the treatment receives the change. I compare a pre-specified outcome between the arms, using a design and analysis that support a causal interpretation.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If I test a new card-application screen, I define the outcome as a successful application. The two conversion probabilities are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p_A=\\mathbb{P}(\\text{successful application}\\mid A), \\qquad p_B=\\mathbb{P}(\\text{successful application}\\mid B).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The effect of interest is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\Delta=p_B-p_A.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.1.1)<\/strong> P sub A is the probability of a successful application given control assignment, and p sub B is the probability of a successful application given treatment assignment.<\/p><p><strong>(7.1.2)<\/strong> Delta equals the treatment conversion probability p sub B minus the control conversion probability p sub A.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-02", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.2 · Why Randomization Matters", "Question": "Why is randomization important in an A/B test?", "Short": "<p>Randomization makes treatment assignment independent of potential outcomes in a simple randomized experiment. This prevents systematic differences in who receives treatment from confounding the comparison. It balances groups in expectation, although chance imbalances can remain in a particular sample.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Randomization creates a fair comparison by assigning treatment independently of the characteristics that would otherwise influence selection into the arms. To express the assignment, I define a binary indicator:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T_i=\\begin{cases} 0,&amp;\\text{control},\\\\ 1,&amp;\\text{treatment}. \\end{cases}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>In a simple 50/50 experiment,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(T_i=1)=\\mathbb{P}(T_i=0)=\\frac12.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>In this simple design, assignment is independent of pre-treatment covariates X sub i:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{T_i\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp X_i.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.3.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>More fundamentally, under random assignment,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T_i\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp(Y_i(0),Y_i(1)).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.3.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This independence breaks systematic confounding between treatment and user characteristics. It is a property of the assignment mechanism, not a promise that the observed group means will be identical.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If version <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is shown only to Android users and version <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)<\/span> only to iOS users, an observed conversion difference cannot be attributed uniquely to the design because operating system and treatment are confounded.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.3.1)<\/strong> T sub i equals zero for control and one for treatment.<\/p><p><strong>(7.3.2)<\/strong> The probability that T sub i equals one and the probability that it equals zero are both one half.<\/p><p><strong>(7.3.3)<\/strong> T sub i is independent of X sub i.<\/p><p><strong>(7.3.4)<\/strong> Treatment assignment T sub i is independent of the pair of potential outcomes Y sub i of zero and Y sub i of one.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-03", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.3 · Unit of Randomization and Unit of Analysis", "Question": "What are the unit of randomization and the unit of analysis?", "Short": "<p>The unit of randomization is the entity assigned to an experimental arm. The unit of analysis is the level at which outcomes are analyzed. They can differ, but the uncertainty calculation must account for dependence within randomized units.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The unit of randomization determines where treatment assignment is independent: it may be a user, account, device, store, city, or company. I choose it based on how treatment is delivered and where interference is likely.<\/p>\n<p>The unit of analysis determines what an observation in the analysis represents. If I analyze a finer level than I randomized, those observations may share a treatment assignment and be correlated. I must account for that clustering rather than count every row as independent evidence.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If users are randomized but each user generates 100 transactions, treating all transactions as 100 independent experimental units can severely underestimate uncertainty because transactions from the same user are correlated. One solution is to aggregate to a user-level metric or use cluster-aware inference.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-04", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.4 · Primary, Secondary, and Guardrail Metrics", "Question": "What are primary, secondary, and guardrail metrics?", "Short": "<p>The primary metric is the pre-specified outcome used to judge experiment success. Secondary metrics explain mechanisms or other consequences. Guardrail metrics track outcomes that should not deteriorate materially, even when the primary metric improves.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>An experiment needs metrics with clear decision roles. I choose the primary metric before seeing the results so that success is judged against the original product question.<\/p>\n<p>I use secondary metrics to understand why the primary outcome changed and to explore additional consequences.<\/p>\n<p>I also define guardrail metrics to capture unacceptable side effects. A favorable primary result does not justify shipping if the change materially harms these outcomes.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>For a card-application interface, I might choose successful application conversion as the primary metric. Form completion time and abandonment by step would help explain the mechanism, while fraud rate, support contacts, crashes, and latency would serve as guardrails.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>A non-significant guardrail result does not establish absence of harm. To demonstrate that deterioration is acceptably small, pre-specify a non-inferiority margin and use the appropriate confidence bound or test.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification-1\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>Pre-specifying metrics reduces the temptation to search through many outcomes after observing the data and report whichever one happens to be significant.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-05", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.5 · Hypotheses for a Conversion Experiment", "Question": "How do you formulate hypotheses for a conversion experiment?", "Short": "<p>For a two-sided conversion experiment, the null states that the treatment and control conversion probabilities are equal, while the alternative states that they differ. For a pre-specified improvement test, the null is a nonpositive treatment effect and the alternative is a positive effect.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The hypotheses for a conversion experiment should reflect the decision question. I define delta as the treatment conversion probability minus the control probability. If either direction matters, the null of equal conversion probabilities is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ H_0:p_A=p_B \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad H_0:\\Delta=0, }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ H_1:p_A\\ne p_B \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad H_1:\\Delta\\ne0. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The alternative above allows either an increase or a decrease. If I instead pre-specify that I am testing for an improvement, I use<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\Delta\\le0, \\qquad H_1:\\Delta&gt;0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.6.1)<\/strong> H naught states that p sub A equals p sub B, equivalently that delta equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(7.6.2)<\/strong> H one states that p sub A is not equal to p sub B, equivalently that delta is not equal to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(7.6.3)<\/strong> For the one-sided test, H naught is delta less than or equal to zero; H one is delta greater than zero.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-06", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.6 · Absolute Effect, Relative Uplift, and Business Thresholds", "Question": "What are absolute effect and relative uplift?", "Short": "<p>An absolute effect is the treatment value minus the control value. For rates, we often express that difference in percentage points. Relative uplift divides the difference by the control value and expresses it as a percentage. A business threshold is the minimum effect worth acting on.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Absolute and relative effects describe the same change on different scales. For example, if conversion increases from ten percent to eleven percent, subtracting the original rate gives the absolute effect:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\Delta=0.11-0.10=0.01,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(+1\\)<\/span> percentage point (pp). The relative uplift is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\frac{0.11-0.10}{0.10}=0.10=10\\%.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I would report this as an increase of one percentage point, or a ten percent relative increase. Naming the scale explicitly avoids confusing percent with percentage points.<\/p>\n<p>A <em>business threshold<\/em> is the smallest effect worth shipping from an economic or operational perspective. It is a decision criterion, not automatically the same thing as a statistical null hypothesis.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.7.1)<\/strong> Delta equals zero point one one minus zero point one zero, which equals zero point zero one.<\/p><p><strong>(7.7.2)<\/strong> Conversion increased from ten percent to eleven percent. That is an absolute increase of one percentage point and a relative increase of ten percent.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-07", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.7 · MDE vs. Business Threshold", "Question": "What is the difference between the minimum detectable effect and the business threshold?", "Short": "<p>The MDE is a design target: the effect at which the experiment is planned to have specified power. A business threshold is a decision target: the smallest effect worth acting on. They may be equal, but significance does not require an observed effect to exceed the MDE.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The MDE and the business threshold answer different questions. When planning, the MDE tells me which true effect I want the test to detect with a chosen probability. When deciding, the business threshold tells me whether an effect would justify implementation costs or other trade-offs. I can align these values, but neither definition implies the other.<\/p>\n<p>A significant estimate smaller than the MDE is possible. The MDE does not mean that every significant effect must be at least that large; it means that the experiment was designed to have the stated power when the true effect equals that size.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-08", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.8 · Sample Size for Two Independent Proportions", "Question": "How do you calculate the required sample size for a conversion A/B test?", "Short": "<p>The sample size for comparing two proportions depends on the baseline rate, target effect, significance level, desired power, and allocation. With equal groups and a small effect, the requirement per group is approximately twice the Bernoulli variance times a squared sum of normal quantiles, divided by the squared effect.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>To plan a two-proportion experiment, I specify a baseline probability p sub one and a target treatment probability p sub two. Suppose both arms will have n users. I first define their average probability:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\bar p=\\frac{p_1+p_2}{2}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A standard large-sample approximation combines the critical threshold under the null with the variability under the alternative. The resulting requirement is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ n\\approx \\frac{ \\left[ z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\sqrt{2\\bar p(1-\\bar p)} + z_{1-\\beta}\\sqrt{p_1(1-p_1)+p_2(1-p_2)} \\right]^2 }{(p_2-p_1)^2} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>per group.<\/p>\n<p>The first term calibrates the significance threshold using the null variance, while the second supplies the power requirement using variability under the specified alternative. The entire expression is a per-group requirement.<\/p>\n<p>For a small effect, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_1\\approx p_2\\approx p\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta=p_2-p_1\\)<\/span>, this simplifies to<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ n\\approx \\frac{2p(1-p) (z_{1-\\alpha/2}+z_{1-\\beta})^2} {\\delta^2}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This displays the main scaling law<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[n\\propto\\frac1{\\delta^2}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Halving the MDE therefore requires roughly four times the data under this approximation. I round the calculated group size upward and plan duration separately.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>With baseline <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=0.10\\)<\/span>, MDE <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta=0.01\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=0.05\\)<\/span>, and power <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.80\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[z_{0.975}\\approx1.96, \\qquad z_{0.8}\\approx0.84.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The simplified formula gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[n\\approx \\frac{2(0.1)(0.9)(1.96+0.84)^2}{0.01^2} \\approx14{,}112\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.11.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>users per group.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.11.1)<\/strong> P bar equals p sub one plus p sub two, all divided by two.<\/p><p><strong>(7.11.2)<\/strong> The sample size per group is approximately the square of a sum, divided by the squared difference in proportions. The first term in the sum is the z quantile at one minus alpha over two, times the square root of two p bar times one minus p bar. The second is the z quantile at one minus beta, times the square root of p sub one times one minus p sub one plus p sub two times one minus p sub two.<\/p><p><strong>(7.11.3)<\/strong> N per group is approximately two p times one minus p, multiplied by the square of the sum of the z quantiles at one minus alpha over two and one minus beta, all divided by delta squared.<\/p><p><strong>(7.11.4)<\/strong> Sample size is proportional to one over delta squared.<\/p><p><strong>(7.11.5)<\/strong> The standard normal quantile at zero point nine seven five is approximately one point nine six, and the quantile at zero point eight is approximately zero point eight four.<\/p><p><strong>(7.11.6)<\/strong> The approximate sample size per group is two times zero point one times zero point nine, multiplied by the square of one point nine six plus zero point eight four, all divided by zero point zero one squared. This gives about fourteen thousand one hundred and twelve users per group.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-09", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.9 · Why Conversion Is Bernoulli/Binomial", "Question": "Why can conversion be modeled using Bernoulli and binomial distributions?", "Short": "<p>A single user’s conversion is a binary outcome, so it can be modeled as Bernoulli. With independent users sharing the same conversion probability, the total number of conversions is binomial. The observed conversion rate is their sample mean, whose distribution can be approximated by a normal distribution in large samples.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Conversion connects a binary observation to a count and then to an estimated rate. I first represent each user’s outcome by an indicator:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_i=\\begin{cases} 1,&amp;\\text{conversion},\\\\ 0,&amp;\\text{no conversion}. \\end{cases}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Under the iid Bernoulli model (independent users with common conversion probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>),<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_i\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and the total number of conversions in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> users is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S=\\sum_iX_i\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Dividing that count by the number of users gives the sample conversion rate:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat p=\\frac Sn=\\bar X,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>with<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[\\hat p]=p, \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\hat p)=\\frac{p(1-p)}n.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For large samples satisfying the normal-approximation conditions, the central limit theorem gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat p\\approx\\mathcal{N}\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}n\\right). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.9.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus the raw observations are Bernoulli/binomial, while the z-test arises from a normal approximation to the estimated proportions.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.9.1)<\/strong> X sub i equals one if user i converts and zero otherwise.<\/p><p><strong>(7.9.2)<\/strong> X sub i follows a Bernoulli distribution with success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(7.9.3)<\/strong> Each X sub i is Bernoulli with success probability p. Their sum S follows a binomial distribution with n trials and success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(7.9.4)<\/strong> P hat equals S divided by n, which equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(7.9.5)<\/strong> The expected value of p hat is p, and its variance is p times one minus p divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(7.9.6)<\/strong> For large n, p hat is approximately normal with mean p and variance p times one minus p divided by n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-10", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.10 · Two-Proportion z-Test", "Question": "What is a two-proportion z-test?", "Short": "<p>A two-proportion z-test compares two independent population probabilities using the observed difference in sample proportions divided by its estimated standard error under the equality null. The null standard error uses a pooled proportion. In large samples, I compare the statistic with a standard normal distribution.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The two-proportion z-test starts I estimate their conversion probabilities by independent conversion counts from control and treatment. I model them as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[x_A\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n_A,p_A), \\qquad x_B\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n_B,p_B),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>with<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\hat p_A=\\frac{x_A}{n_A}, \\qquad \\hat p_B=\\frac{x_B}{n_B}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The equality null states that<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:p_A=p_B,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Under this null there is a common conversion probability, so I estimate it by pooling successes and users across both groups:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat p=\\frac{x_A+x_B}{n_A+n_B}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Substituting the pooled estimate into the null variance gives the null standard error:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ SE_0= \\sqrt{ \\hat p(1-\\hat p) \\left(\\frac1{n_A}+\\frac1{n_B}\\right) }. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I then standardize the observed difference to obtain the z-statistic:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ Z=\\frac{\\hat p_B-\\hat p_A}{SE_0}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a two-sided test,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p=2[1-\\Phi(|Z|)].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.14.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\le\\alpha\\)<\/span>, reject the null of equal conversion rates. This is a large-sample approximation requiring independent groups and adequate success/failure counts in both arms. With sparse counts or rates near the boundaries, normal calibration can fail.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.14.1)<\/strong> The control count follows a binomial distribution with n sub A trials and probability p sub A, while the treatment count has n sub B trials and probability p sub B.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.2)<\/strong> P hat sub A equals x sub A divided by n sub A, and p hat sub B equals x sub B divided by n sub B.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.3)<\/strong> H naught states that p sub A equals p sub B.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.4)<\/strong> The pooled estimate p hat is x sub A plus x sub B, all divided by n sub A plus n sub B.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.5)<\/strong> The null standard error is the square root of p hat times one minus p hat, multiplied by the quantity one over n sub A plus one over n sub B. The entire product is inside the square root.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.6)<\/strong> Z is p hat sub B minus p hat sub A, all divided by the null standard error.<\/p><p><strong>(7.14.7)<\/strong> The two-sided p-value is two times the quantity one minus capital Phi evaluated at the absolute value of Z.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-11", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.11 · Experiment Validation: SRM and Balance", "Question": "What is sample ratio mismatch?", "Short": "<p>Sample ratio mismatch is an allocation imbalance too large to plausibly explain by the intended randomization mechanism. It can signal assignment, logging, or selection problems, so I investigate it before trusting the estimated treatment effect. Covariate balance checks complement SRM but do not require exactly identical groups.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Sample ratio mismatch checks whether observed group sizes are consistent with the planned assignment ratio. For example, in an intended fifty-fifty split, sixty thousand control users and forty thousand treatment users would be a strong warning sign. I would investigate the assignment and data pipeline before interpreting the outcome comparison.<\/p>\n<p>I would consider broken randomization, differential logging, treatment-specific crashes, platform filters, and post-assignment exclusions as possible causes. A chi-square goodness-of-fit test is a common diagnostic when its count assumptions are appropriate.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"balance-checks.\">Balance checks.<\/h4>\n<p>Pre-treatment characteristics such as country, device, historical spend, or age may also be compared. Small imbalances occur naturally under randomization; the goal is to identify systematic implementation failures, not to demand identical groups.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-12", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.12 · A/A Tests", "Question": "What is an A/A test, and why is it useful?", "Short": "<p>An A/A test randomly assigns units to groups that receive the same treatment. Because there should be no causal effect, it helps diagnose the experiment pipeline and the calibration of inference. Repeated calibrated tests should produce false positives at roughly the nominal rate, subject to sampling variability.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>An A/A test checks the experimentation system without intentionally changing the experience between groups. I still randomize and run the usual analysis, but both arms receive the same treatment. The expected causal comparison is therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\Delta=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.13.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This setup lets me check randomization, assignment logging, metric definitions, SRM detection, and false-positive calibration using a case with no intended effect.<\/p>\n<p>If many independent A/A tests are run at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha=0.05\\)<\/span>, approximately 5% should be significant purely by chance under a well-calibrated continuous testing procedure. A much larger rate suggests a broken experimentation pipeline or invalid inference assumptions.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.13.1)<\/strong> In an A/A test, the true treatment effect delta equals zero.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-13", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.13 · What to Do After a Statistically Significant Result", "Question": "What should you check after obtaining a statistically significant result?", "Short": "<p>After a statistically significant result, I check experiment validity, guardrails, and multiplicity, then assess the estimated effect and confidence interval against the business threshold. Rejecting a zero-effect null does not establish that the effect is large enough to justify shipping.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A statistically significant result is one input to a decision. It says that the data meet the test’s criterion for incompatibility with a null such as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[H_0:\\Delta=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>It does not by itself answer whether the effect is large enough to justify shipping the change.<\/p>\n<p>To assess practical value, I introduce a separate decision threshold. Suppose the business requires at least<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\Delta_{\\mathrm{business}}=1\\text{ pp}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The interval<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[[0.2,1.4]\\text{ pp}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>excludes zero, so a positive effect is statistically supported, but it includes values both below and above the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> pp business threshold. Therefore the data do not establish that the effect exceeds the threshold.<\/p>\n<p>I separate three questions so that the statistical result is not confused with the product decision:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\text{p-value: Is the effect distinguishable from the null?} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\text{confidence interval: Which effect sizes are compatible with the data?} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\text{business threshold: Is the effect large enough to matter?} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(7.16.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a stable data-generating process with the relevant variance assumptions, more data reduce the standard error at the usual rate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(SE\\propto1/\\sqrt n\\)<\/span>. They do not increase the true effect. If the true effect is about <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.8\\)<\/span> pp, an increasingly precise interval will eventually concentrate around <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.8\\)<\/span> pp and may establish that the effect is real but practically too small.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(7.16.1)<\/strong> H naught states that delta equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(7.16.2)<\/strong> The business threshold for delta is one percentage point.<\/p><p><strong>(7.16.3)<\/strong> The interval runs from zero point two to one point four percentage points.<\/p><p><strong>(7.16.4)<\/strong> The p-value asks whether the effect is statistically distinguishable from the null.<\/p><p><strong>(7.16.5)<\/strong> The confidence interval describes which effect sizes are compatible with the data under the procedure.<\/p><p><strong>(7.16.6)<\/strong> The business threshold asks whether the effect is large enough to matter.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s7-14", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.14 · Peeking and Stopping Rules", "Question": "Why does naive peeking increase the false-positive rate?", "Short": "<p>Naive peeking is repeatedly checking an ordinary fixed-horizon test and stopping when it becomes significant. This increases false positives because the procedure has multiple opportunities to reject. I either analyze once at a pre-specified horizon or use inference designed to remain valid under the planned sequential monitoring.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Naive peeking means repeatedly checking an ordinary fixed-horizon p-value and stopping the first time it crosses the significance threshold.<\/p>\n<p>A fixed-horizon test controls Type I error for its pre-specified analysis procedure. Repeated opportunities to stop after favorable fluctuations increase the chance of eventually crossing the threshold even under a true null, inflating the actual false-positive rate.<\/p>\n<p>I handle this in one of two ways. For a fixed-horizon analysis, I pre-specify a sample size or calendar horizon independently of observed treatment effects and analyze once as planned. If I need interim decisions, I choose an appropriate sequential method, such as group-sequential testing, alpha-spending, an SPRT-type procedure, or always-valid inference, and follow that method’s stopping rules.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>Merely writing down an outcome-dependent stopping rule in advance does not make a fixed-horizon test valid. Extending a test after inspecting its result also requires an inference procedure valid for that adaptation.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-15", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.15 · Minimum Duration and Time Effects", "Question": "How should you choose the duration of an A/B test?", "Short": "<p>An experiment’s duration must capture the time patterns relevant to its target effect, not merely deliver enough observations. Weekday cycles, delayed conversions, novelty, and user learning can change what the measured effect represents. I therefore plan both sample size and duration.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Minimum duration determines which time-dependent behavior an experiment captures. Reaching the numerical sample-size target is not always sufficient. Experiments may need to span relevant temporal cycles because outcomes can depend on weekday/weekend behavior, salary dates, delayed conversions, novelty effects, or learning effects.<\/p>\n<p>I would specify both a minimum sample size and a duration that covers relevant cycles and observation windows.<\/p>\n<p>Two effects help explain why early results may not reflect a stable long-run impact. A novelty effect is a temporary response caused by the newness of a treatment rather than its stable long-run value. A <em>learning effect<\/em> occurs when users need time to adapt to a changed interface or process.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-16", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.16 · Multiple Variants and Multiple Testing", "Question": "How do multiple variants affect multiple testing and statistical power?", "Short": "<p>Testing several variants against a control creates multiple comparisons and divides traffic across more arms. I account for multiplicity according to the decision objective and calculate sample sizes for the actual allocation. Otherwise, false discoveries can increase while power for each comparison decreases.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Multiple variants change both inference and experiment design. If I compare a control with twenty variants using separate five percent tests, the family-wise chance of a false discovery can become large. Corrections such as Bonferroni or Holm may be required depending on the decision objective.<\/p>\n<p>I also have to account for how the variants divide traffic. With fixed total traffic, fewer users per arm imply larger standard errors and lower power. Thus many simultaneous variants create both a multiplicity problem and a sample-allocation problem.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}, {"ID": "s7-17", "Deck": "07", "Topic": "7.17 · A/B Test End-to-End Checklist", "Question": "How do you run an A/B test from start to finish?", "Short": "<p>To run an A/B test, I define the decision, treatment, randomization unit, and metrics; pre-specify hypotheses and sample size; validate the experiment; and estimate the effect with uncertainty. I then assess guardrails and practical value before deciding what to do.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>An A/B test starts with a product decision and a causal question. I first define the control and treatment experiences, the eligible population, and the units of randomization and analysis. I then choose a primary metric that answers the product question and guardrails that capture unacceptable side effects.<\/p>\n<p>Before looking at outcomes, I specify the null and alternative hypotheses, significance level, desired power, minimum detectable effect, and baseline rate or variance. Using the actual traffic allocation, I calculate sample sizes and choose a minimum duration that covers relevant cycles and conversion windows. I also decide whether analysis will occur once at a fixed horizon or follow a valid sequential procedure.<\/p>\n<p>During implementation, I randomize consistently and record assignment, exposure, and outcomes. Before interpreting a treatment effect, I investigate sample ratio mismatch, logging problems, unexpected exclusions, and other signs that the planned comparison has been compromised.<\/p>\n<p>For analysis, I estimate the treatment effect and its standard error at the appropriate independence level. I report a confidence interval and the p-value for the pre-specified test, applying the planned treatment of multiplicity. I also check guardrails and explain any difference between statistical significance and practical importance.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, I compare the plausible effect sizes with the business threshold, costs, and risks. I recommend shipping, rejecting the change, or gathering more information according to that evidence. If more data are needed after inspecting results, I use a procedure valid for that adaptation rather than extending an ordinary fixed-horizon test until it becomes significant.<\/p>\n", "Readings": ""}]}}};
